7 false] [17592186119537 1145 "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,{{(LU)}_{⏟}}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $U\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{y},$ we obtain\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14063081/assets/b521c0b31d05de273d0abd37e00b046c0e36ed47ef30d22784a24cace4d676f9.png)\n\nNow, we solve the two systems in turn.\n\n- First, we solve $L\\mathbf{y} = \\mathbf{b}.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14063081/assets/9dcc9f71745cce72ce7e122b3bc8cd7064d05f3be90d712202f42956956ef18e.png)\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}=6 \\\\ -2{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}=-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is $y_1=6$ and $y_2=4.$ Therefore, \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14063081/assets/903fbfe6828526dace264d65baebf58c31e36db8a54e25ed1f3be859e6079ebb.png)\n\n- Next, we solve $U\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{y}.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14063081/assets/dcb234ae13cab2676ea5afa7fde452c870ff5088ff5748342c517bf2a96327a8.png)\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}7{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=6 \\\\ -2{x}_{2}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is $x_2=-2$ and $x_1=2.$ Therefore, \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14063081/assets/53bace542ba4dfd0d7e5e4bf9b6451c7f39502dd1bf24d030f654050629f0e0d.png)\n\nFinally, $x_1 \\cdot y_2 = 2 \\cdot 4 = 8.$" 13194139535337 true] [17592186119538 1100 "Consider the matrix $L$ and the vector $\\mathbf b$, given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nIf $A=LU$ is the LU factorization of some $3\\times 3$ matrix $A$, find the vector $\\mathbf{y}$ such that\n\n${Ly = b; Ux = y$\n\nis equivalent to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$." 13194139535337 false] [17592186119538 1100 "Consider the matrix $L$ and the vector $\\mathbf b,$ given by \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14063081/assets/f88cbbb9459dd3b467e6b87d230ad23634461597aa9d8da3e933774273e2ab2f.png)\nIf $A=LU$ is the LU factorization of some $3\\times 3$ matrix $A,$ find the vector $\\mathbf{y}$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}Ly=b \\\\ Ux=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}.$" 13194139535337 true] [17592186119538 1145 "First, notice that since $A=LU$, we can write\n\n$$\nAx = b⟹(LU)_(⏟)_(A)x = b\n$$\n\nNow, setting\n\n$$\nU\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{y}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{Ly = b; Ux = y\n$$\n\nWe now solve\n\n$$\nL\\mathbf{y} = \\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nWriting this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n{y_{1} = 2; y_{1} + y_{2} = 4; y_{1} - 2y_{2} + y_{3} = 1\n$$\n\nWe can compute the solution to this system using forward substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{1} &= 2, \\\\\ny_{2} &= 4 - y_{1} \\\\\n&= 4 - (2) \\\\\n&= 2, \\\\\ny_{3} &= 1 - y_{1} + 2y_{2} \\\\\n&= 1 - (2) + 2(2) \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139535337 false] [17592186119538 1145 "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,{{(LU)}_{⏟}}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $U\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{y},$ we obtain\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14063081/assets/7704f9285f14f496bb9f1721d188866c9464c585e33235c5cc6eee42e3f3a3eb.png)\n\nWe now solve $L\\mathbf{y} = \\mathbf{b}.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14063081/assets/712643d674cf8a7a086a9a935659d86bba7bd2fb25f131ec56e798cfc1d538d9.png)\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}=2 \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}=4 \\\\ {y}_{1}-2{y}_{2}+{y}_{3}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can compute the solution to this system using forward substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & =2, \\\\ {y}_{2} & =4-{y}_{1} \\\\  & =4-(2) \\\\  & =2, \\\\ {y}_{3} & =1-{y}_{1}+2{y}_{2} \\\\  & =1-(2)+2(2) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14063081/assets/151dc664a131df0f3e13f2997bb1af6d41586b5f4dcf8a1acd1342f85e1b9c92.png)" 13194139535337 true] [17592186119630 1100 "Consider the matrix $L$ and the vector $\\mathbf b$, given by\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 15 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nIf $A=LU$ is the LU factorization of some $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$, find the vector $\\mathbf{y}$ such that\n\n${Ly = b; Ux = y$\nis equivalent to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$." 13194139535337 false] [17592186119630 1100 "Consider the matrix $L$ and the vector $\\mathbf b,$ given by \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14063081/assets/fd1cf7c3d929fb79576a59d4ef3aebd3f24a0d570c938ab80ebd44c5bd4c238f.png)\n\nIf $A=LU$ is the LU factorization of some $2\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ find the vector $\\mathbf{y}$ such that \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}Ly=b \\\\ Ux=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}.$" 13194139535337 true] [17592186119630 1145 "First, notice that since $A=LU$, we can write\n\n$$\nAx = b⟹(LU)_(⏟)_(A)x = b\n$$\n\nNow, setting\n\n$$\nU\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{y}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{Ly = b; Ux = y\n$$\n\nWe now solve\n\n$$\nL\\mathbf{y} = \\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nWriting this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 15 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}y_{1} \\\\ y_{2}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 66\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n{y_{1} = 5; 15y_{1} + y_{2} = 66\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is $y_1=5$ and $y_2=-9$.\n\nTherefore, $\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139535337 false] [17592186119630 1145 "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,{{(LU)}_{⏟}}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $U\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{y},$ we obtain\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14063081/assets/b521c0b31d05de273d0abd37e00b046c0e36ed47ef30d22784a24cace4d676f9.png)\n\nWe now solve $L\\mathbf{y} = \\mathbf{b}.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 15 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 66\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}=5 \\\\ 15{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}=66.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is $y_1=5$ and $y_2=-9.$ \n\nTherefore, ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14063081/assets/ccb4ab47d3a270cf9545d4f79e6f90b944cf6121fca3752eeee6ddc712fcfdec.png)" 13194139535337 true] [17592186119723 1020 #edb/ref 17592186344304 13194139535337 true] [17592186119723 1020 #edb/ref 17592186344346 13194139535337 true] [17592186119723 1020 #edb/ref 17592186344353 13194139535337 true] [17592186119723 1020 #edb/ref 17592186344381 13194139535337 true] [17592186119724 1020 #edb/ref 17592186344325 13194139535337 true] [17592186119724 1020 #edb/ref 17592186344332 13194139535337 true] [17592186119724 1020 #edb/ref 17592186344339 13194139535337 true] [17592186119724 1020 #edb/ref 17592186344374 13194139535337 true] [17592186119724 1020 #edb/ref 17592186344395 13194139535337 true] [17592186119807 1020 #edb/ref 17592186344311 13194139535337 true] [17592186119807 1020 #edb/ref 17592186344318 13194139535337 true] [17592186119807 1020 #edb/ref 17592186344360 13194139535337 true] [17592186119807 1020 #edb/ref 17592186344367 13194139535337 true] [17592186119807 1020 #edb/ref 17592186344388 13194139535337 true] [17592186120005 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535337 true] [17592186120005 1100 "Consider the LU decomposition of the matrix $A$ and the vector $b$, given by\n\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(L)\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(U), b = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$.\nIf $⟨x_{1}, x_{2}⟩$ and $⟨y_{1}, y_{2}⟩$ are the solutions to the systems $Ax = b$ and $Ly = b$ respectively, what is the value of $x_{1} \\cdot y_{1}$?" 13194139535337 false] [17592186120005 1100 "Consider the LU decomposition of the matrix $A$ and the vector $b,$ given by\n\n$$\nA={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U},\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf $⟨{x}_{1},{x}_{2}⟩$ and $⟨{y}_{1},{y}_{2}⟩$ are the solutions to the systems $Ax=b$ and $Ly=b$ respectively, what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {y}_{1}?$" 13194139535337 true] [17592186120005 1145 "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,{{(LU)}_{⏟}}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $Ux=y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nL{{(Ux)}_{⏟}}_{y}=b\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}Ly=b \\\\ Ux=y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the two systems in turn.\n\n- First, we solve $Ly=b.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=b=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}=-1 \\\\ -{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is ${y}_{1}=-1$ and ${y}_{2}=2.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\ny=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- Next, we solve $Ux=y.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=y=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=-1 \\\\ {x}_{2}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is ${x}_{2}=2$ and ${x}_{1}=-1.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}\\cdot {y}_{1}=(-1)\\cdot (-1)=1.$" 13194139535337 true] [17592186344304 1017 #edb/ref 17592186344305 13194139535337 true] [17592186344304 1089 "q-104861" 13194139535337 true] [17592186344304 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535337 true] [17592186344304 1100 "Consider the LU decomposition of the matrix $A$ and the vector $b,$ given by\n\n$$\nA={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U},\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf $⟨{x}_{1},{x}_{2}⟩$ and $⟨{y}_{1},{y}_{2}⟩$ are the solutions of the systems $Ax=b$ and $Ly=b$ respectively, what is the value of the product ${x}_{1}\\cdot {y}_{2}?$" 13194139535337 true] [17592186344304 1113 #uuid "8b452cf4-4d72-5df1-88c6-867fd46459f0" 13194139535337 true] [17592186344304 1145 "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,{{(LU)}_{⏟}}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $Ux=y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nL{{(Ux)}_{⏟}}_{y}=b\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}Ly=b \\\\ Ux=y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the two systems in turn.\n\n- First, we solve $Ly=b.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}=1 \\\\ -2{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution t